(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-cp-exam-2-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does an adaptive B-cell or T-cell receptor recognize?","choices":["Patterns shared by groups of organisms","Any foreign protein, whatever its shape","A particular epitope","Antigens met in a previous exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adaptive specificity means B-cell and T-cell receptors recognize particular epitopes, the specific regions of an antigen. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which progenitor gives rise to megakaryocytes?","choices":["Lymphoid progenitor","Myeloid progenitor","Erythroid precursor","Granulocyte precursor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the simplified lineage model the multipotent stem cell gives myeloid and lymphoid progenitors, and the myeloid progenitor gives erythroid precursors, megakaryocytes (which make platelets), granulocytes and monocytes. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which factors are associated with dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Amyloidosis","Aortic valve calcification","Streptococcal cross-reactivity","Alcohol, genetics, pregnancy and a post-viral state"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Four causes with nothing obvious in common, which is why the condition is grouped by what the ventricle does rather than by what caused it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What makes vaccination a form of ARTIFICIAL active immunity?","choices":["Antibodies come from another source","The antigen is met through ordinary contact","It acts with no prior sensitization","Antigen exposure is done on purpose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active immunity is acquired by the body's own processes after exposure to the antigen; it is artificial when the exposure is deliberate, as with vaccination. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How is an absolute neutrophil count of 750 cells/µL graded?","choices":["Agranulocytosis","Severe neutropenia","Moderate neutropenia","Mild neutropenia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An absolute neutrophil count of 750 cells/µL falls in the moderate band, 500 to 1,000 cells/µL; severe is below 500 and mild is 1,000 to 1,500. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a neoplasm of which cells?","choices":["Precursor B cells","Mature B cells","Mature T cells","Plasma cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a mature B-cell neoplasm, related to small lymphocytic lymphoma (CLL/SLL). — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 23, 28"},{"q":"What does the mean cell volume measure?","choices":["The average hemoglobin mass per red cell","The hemoglobin concentration in packed cells","The share of blood volume that is red cells","The average volume of a red cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mean cell volume is the average volume of a red cell; it sorts anemias into microcytic (below 80 fL, femtoliters), normocytic (80 to 100 fL) and macrocytic (above 100 fL). — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 33"},{"q":"What is the difference between valvular stenosis and regurgitation?","choices":["Stenosis is failure to close, regurgitation is failure to open","Both describe failure of the valve to open","Stenosis affects only the mitral valve and regurgitation only the aortic","Stenosis is failure to open completely, regurgitation is failure to close completely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence follows directly: one makes the heart push harder, the other makes it push more. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How does mitral stenosis lead to pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["It increases pulmonary blood flow","It causes hypoxic vasoconstriction","It raises left atrial pressure","It raises blood viscosity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis, mitral regurgitation and left ventricular failure cause pulmonary hypertension by increasing left atrial pressure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is Monckeberg medial sclerosis?","choices":["Lipid plaque formation with a necrotic core","Calcification of the media of muscular arteries","Hyaline thickening of arterioles from hypertension","Inflammatory destruction of the vessel wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is calcification of the medial walls of muscular arteries, one of the four patterns of arteriosclerosis, and it is usually not clinically significant. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What defines a dissection?","choices":["Localized abnormal dilation of the vessel","Inflammation of the vessel wall with leukocyte infiltration","Blood enters a wall defect and tunnels through medial planes","Calcification of the media without luminal change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct &mdash; through medial or medial-adventitial planes. The vessel is not merely dilated; its wall has been split into layers by blood under pressure. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause infective endocarditis?","choices":["Coxsackievirus and adenovirus","Candida alone","Group A streptococcus through immune cross-reactivity","Streptococcal strains and Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The same two names recur, with the less virulent organisms reserved for the subacute form on an already damaged valve. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What distinguishes angina pectoris from infarction at the cellular level?","choices":["The ischemia is intermittent and insufficient to kill cardiac cells","The ischemia affects only the right ventricle","There is no ischemia, only abnormal conduction","Cell death occurs but is confined to the endocardium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Same mechanism, different endpoint: the supply falls short but recovers before the cells are lost. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What defines emphysema?","choices":["Alveolar wall destruction without fibrosis","Alveolar wall thickening with fibrosis","Reversible airway inflammation","Permanent dilation of the bronchi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema (type A chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, the “pink puffer”) is destruction of alveolar walls without fibrosis and abnormal enlargement of distal air sacs. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which of these is a T-cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction?","choices":["Anaphylaxis to insect venom","Contact dermatitis","ABO transfusion incompatibility","Serum sickness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contact dermatitis is type IV, mediated by sensitized CD4 or CD8 T cells, along with tuberculosis-related reactions and T-cell-mediated transplant rejection. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which consequences do monoclonal proteins and marrow infiltration cause in myeloma?","choices":["Renal injury, anemia and impaired immunity","Leukostasis in the lungs and brain","Thrombosis with a falling platelet count","Raised hematocrit from plasma loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In multiple myeloma, monoclonal proteins and marrow infiltration can cause renal injury, anemia and impaired immunity; altered bone remodeling adds osteolytic lesions and hypercalcemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What injury starts acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Rupture of subpleural blebs","Damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane","Loss of radial traction on airways","Closure of the collapsible pharynx"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute respiratory distress syndrome begins with damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane, giving widespread alveolar infiltrates and severe dyspnea. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What combination is essential hypertension suspected to arise from?","choices":["Small changes in renal sodium handling and vessel tone","A single inherited defect in the renin gene","Autoimmune destruction of the vascular endothelium","Chronic infection of the arterial wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Essential hypertension is suspected to arise from a combination of small changes in renal sodium homeostasis and in vessel wall tone or structure, so the abnormality is suspected to be distributed across two systems rather than located in one. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which two blood-borne events accompany the neointimal response?","choices":["Red cell aggregation and hemolysis","Eosinophil degranulation and mast cell activation","Plasma cell infiltration with antibody deposition","Platelet activation and leukocyte recruitment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a thrombus forms and the inflammatory cascade begins at the injury site, so the response is simultaneously thrombotic and inflammatory. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is precipitation, as an antibody function?","choices":["Clumping antigen-bearing cells","Cross-linking soluble antigens","Coating a cell for ingestion","Blocking a virus's attachment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies cross-link soluble antigens into larger complexes that can become insoluble and precipitate, which may help phagocytes clear them. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"By what two routes does angiotensin II raise blood pressure?","choices":["Raising heart rate and respiratory rate","Increasing red cell mass and blood viscosity","Dilating the efferent arteriole to raise filtration","Raising smooth muscle tone, and driving aldosterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct &mdash; one route acts on resistance immediately, the other on volume through increased renal sodium resorption, so both determinants of pressure are addressed. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Under which group of restrictive disorders is acute respiratory distress syndrome classified?","choices":["Fibrotic interstitial diseases","Pleural space disorders","Neuromuscular and chest wall disorders","Atelectatic parenchymal disorders"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute (adult) and infant respiratory distress syndromes are the atelectatic disorders of the lung parenchyma. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 67, 68"},{"q":"What does a monocyte become once it enters tissue during inflammation?","choices":["A plasma cell","A mast cell","A neutrophil","A macrophage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes circulate in blood and enter tissues during inflammation, where tissue signals promote differentiation into macrophages. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Atherosclerosis narrows the coronary lumen. Which processes does that narrowing predispose to?","choices":["Valvular calcification and regurgitation","Pericardial effusion and tamponade","Thrombosis, coronary vasospasm and endothelial cell dysfunction","Myocardial leukocyte infiltration and necrosis without vessel involvement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The narrowing is the setting rather than the whole disease: what precipitates an event is one of these three acting on an already narrowed vessel. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which risk factor for deep vein thrombosis reflects reduced blood flow?","choices":["Dehydration","Immobility","Oral estrogen","Surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside increased venous pressure, mechanical injury, increased blood viscosity, anatomic variations and increased coagulability. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"For a patient already hospitalized for another reason, within what time of admission must respiratory symptoms begin for the pneumonia to count as community-acquired?","choices":["Under 12 hours","Under 24 hours","Under 48 hours","Under 96 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients hospitalized for other reasons for under 48 hours before respiratory symptoms develop are also called community-acquired. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 108, 109"},{"q":"From which precursor do lymphocytes arise?","choices":["Myeloblast","Monoblast","Lymphoblast","Megakaryocyte"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In blood cell formation the hematopoietic stem cell gives rise to the lymphoblast, which becomes the lymphocyte. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does the membrane attack complex kill a target cell?","choices":["It triggers apoptosis with granzymes","It disrupts ionic and osmotic balance","It coats the cell for phagocytes","It blocks the cell's receptor binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pores damage the membrane and disrupt ionic and osmotic balance; ions and water move abnormally, and severe disruption leads to cell lysis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 68, 74"},{"q":"Which factors are described as impairing coronary perfusion?","choices":["Plaques, thrombosis, vasospasm, microcirculation, pressure","Increased heart rate and contractility, acting alone","Valvular stenosis and regurgitation, acting alone","Pericardial inflammation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Five different ways to arrive at the same shortfall, only one of which is the fixed narrowing people picture first. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the central hemodynamic abnormality in mitral stenosis?","choices":["An abnormal gradient during systole","An abnormal left atrial to left ventricular pressure gradient during diastole","Backflow from the ventricle into the atrium during systole","Obstruction to outflow from the left ventricle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The atrium stays at higher pressure than the ventricle through diastole, and the gradient widens as the stenosis worsens. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which pair of antibody effects acts on a soluble toxin?","choices":["Opsonization and lysis","Agglutination and opsonization","Agglutination and lysis","Neutralization and precipitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A toxin is soluble, so antibodies neutralize its action and cross-link toxin molecules into precipitating complexes; viruses are neutralized and agglutinated, bacteria opsonized. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 54, 58"},{"q":"Which anemia is listed in the microcytic group?","choices":["Sideroblastic anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Anemia of marrow failure","Anemia of renal disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The microcytic group (mean cell volume below 80 fL, femtoliters) lists iron deficiency, thalassemia, inflammation in some cases, and sideroblastic anemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A serosanguinous pericardial effusion is secondary to which causes?","choices":["Heart failure and hypoproteinemia together","Obstruction to lymphatic drainage alone","Penetrating cardiac trauma only","Blunt trauma, cardiac surgery or resuscitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three involve mechanical force on the chest, which is what puts blood into what would otherwise be a serous fluid. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"After initiation, which enzyme-cofactor pair activates factor X?","choices":["Factor IXa with factor VIIIa","Factor Xa with factor Va","Thrombin with factor XIIIa","Plasmin with protein S"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the factor X activation step, IXa with its cofactor VIIIa activates X to Xa; Xa with Va then generates thrombin. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"Which tissues have the highest density of capillaries?","choices":["Skin and subcutaneous fat","Myocardium and brain","Tendon and ligament","Cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because capillary density tracks metabolic rate &mdash; tissues that consume the most oxygen need the shortest diffusion distances to reach it. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which vasculitis is classified as large vessel?","choices":["Polyarteritis nodosa","Kawasaki disease","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Giant cell arteritis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polyarteritis nodosa and Kawasaki disease are medium vessel, and the small vessel group splits into ANCA-associated and immune complex subgroups. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What two quantities determine blood pressure?","choices":["Vascular resistance and cardiac output","Blood viscosity and vessel length","Heart rate and respiratory rate","Hemoglobin concentration and oxygen saturation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resistance is regulated at the arterioles by neural and hormonal input; output is heart rate times stroke volume, with stroke volume influenced by blood volume. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which step follows adherence of the microbe to the phagocyte?","choices":["Pseudopods engulf the particle","Lysosome fuses with the vesicle","Residual material is exocytosed","Acid hydrolases digest the microbe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sequence is: the microbe adheres; pseudopods engulf it into a phagosome; the phagosome fuses with a lysosome; enzymes digest it; residual material leaves by exocytosis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is extravascular hemolysis?","choices":["Red-cell rupture within the circulation","Premature red-cell destruction within phagocytes","Red-cell loss from bleeding vessels","Failure of red-cell production in marrow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the majority of hemolytic anemias the premature destruction of red cells occurs within phagocytes, the same cells that normally remove old red cells; this is called extravascular hemolysis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 39"},{"q":"Why does a clonal result alone not establish malignancy?","choices":["Clonal cells carry many different rearrangements","PCR cannot detect receptor-gene rearrangements","Malignant lymphoid cells are usually polyclonal","Reactive or premalignant cells can also be clonal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A clone is a population descended from a common progenitor, and reactive or premalignant populations can also be clonal. The result must be interpreted with morphology, immunophenotype and clinical findings. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"Why is severe combined immunodeficiency called combined?","choices":["It combines primary and secondary causes","It affects both complement and antibody","T-cell and B-cell immunity both fail","It joins allergy with immune deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe combined immunodeficiency is profound T-cell dysfunction with impaired B-cell immunity; it may present in early infancy or be found by newborn screening. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What does the PT (prothrombin time) assess?","choices":["Extrinsic factor VII and the common factors","Intrinsic factors XII, XI, IX and VIII","Plasmin's breakdown of fibrin","Platelet adhesion via von Willebrand factor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The PT (prothrombin time) assesses the extrinsic factor (VII) and the common factors (X, V, II and I); the aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time) assesses the intrinsic factors XII, XI, IX and VIII plus the common ones. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Across how many thin layers of the respiratory membrane do oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse?","choices":["Two","Four","Six","Eight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse across six thin layers: alveolar fluid with surfactant, alveolar epithelium, its basement membrane, the interstitial space, the capillary basement membrane and the capillary endothelium. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How do changes in older adults affect immune responses?","choices":["Weaker responses to new antigens","Stronger responses to new antigens","Disappearance of autoantibodies","Return of maternal IgG protection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In older adults, changes in lymphocyte function and repertoire can reduce responses to new antigens, and autoantibodies become more common. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which category of acute respiratory failure shows hypoxemia with a normal or low carbon dioxide?","choices":["Hypercarbic ventilatory failure","Hypoxic respiratory failure","Combined respiratory and ventilatory failure","Acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Failure of respiration or oxygenation gives hypoxemia with normal or lower carbon dioxide: hypoxic respiratory failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are esophageal varices related to?","choices":["Cirrhosis and portal venous hypertension","Valve insufficiency in the lower extremities","Deep vein thrombosis of the femoral vein","Atherosclerosis of the celiac artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct &mdash; dilated submucosal distal esophageal veins. Hemorrhoids are the equivalent process at the anorectal junction. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"At what degree of occlusion is blood flow described as compromised?","choices":["Twenty-five percent","Fifty percent","Seventy-five percent","Ninety-five percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It takes a great deal of narrowing before flow suffers, which is why plaques grow silently for years. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What links each obstructive event to cardiac and cerebrovascular disturbance?","choices":["Asphyxia with sudden autonomic arousal","Immune complexes in vessel walls","Emboli from the pharynx","Steady hyperventilation through the night"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With each obstruction, progressive asphyxia (lack of oxygen, excess carbon dioxide) and sudden autonomic and behavioral arousal result in acute cardiac and cerebrovascular disturbances. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 102, 103"},{"q":"What do the capillaries exchange?","choices":["Oxygen alone","Fluid, nutrients, electrolytes and wastes","Blood cells into the interstitium","Nothing &mdash; capillaries simply conduct blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Capillaries exchange fluid, nutrients, electrolytes, hormones and waste products between blood and tissues, in both directions. Their thin walls and slow flow favor this exchange of diffusible substances. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Antibodies formed against inhaled organic particles in hypersensitivity pneumonitis produce which hypersensitivity type?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies produced to inhaled organic particles cause a type III reaction: antigen-antibody complexes deposit in the lung, initiating inflammation and granulomas. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"On the myeloid branch, which neoplasm arises from the red-cell line?","choices":["Polycythemia vera","Essential thrombocythemia","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Monocytic leukemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the division of hematologic neoplasms by lineage, the red-cell line of the myeloid stem cell gives polycythemia vera and erythroid leukemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Macrocytic anemias often stem from abnormalities that impair what?","choices":["Hemoglobin synthesis","Red-cell survival in the blood","Plasma volume regulation","Erythroid precursor maturation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrocytic anemias often stem from abnormalities that impair the maturation of erythroid precursors in the bone marrow, as in the megaloblastic anemias of vitamin B12 or folate deficiency. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33"},{"q":"Where are nearly all myocardial infarcts located, and which artery is most often responsible?","choices":["The left ventricular walls, most often the left anterior descending","The right ventricle, most often the right coronary artery","The atria, most often the circumflex","The interventricular septum only, most often the circumflex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The left ventricle does the pressure work, so it is the chamber whose supply matters most, and one artery accounts for the largest share. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What initiates atherosclerotic plaque formation?","choices":["Deposition of calcium on the intimal surface","Rupture of the vasa vasorum within the arterial wall","Platelet aggregation on an intact endothelium","Injury to the endothelial cells of the vessel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Everything that follows -- permeability, leukocytes, oxidized lipid, foam cells -- is downstream of that first injury. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What mediates the immediate phase of allergic (extrinsic) asthma?","choices":["Sensitized T cells","Complement membrane pores","IgE on mast cells","Neutrophil proteases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The immediate phase is mediated by IgE to specific antigens; IgE binds mast cells, which release chemical mediators when the antigen arrives. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the basic lesion of bronchiectasis?","choices":["Alveolar destruction without fibrosis","Reversible smooth muscle spasm","Wall destruction that dilates the bronchi","Gland hypertrophy narrowing the lumen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In bronchiectasis destruction of the bronchial walls results in dilation of the bronchi: an obstructive, suppurative disorder. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Why is the pulmonary edema of acute respiratory distress syndrome called noncardiogenic?","choices":["It comes from left atrial pressure","It comes from low plasma oncotic pressure","It comes from leaky capillaries","It is a low-protein transudate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Injury to the alveolar-capillary membrane increases vascular permeability, flooding the alveoli with protein-rich fluid: noncardiogenic pulmonary edema from leaky capillaries. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 84, 85"},{"q":"What forms the antigen-binding site of an antibody?","choices":["The light-chain constant region alone","Heavy and light variable regions together","The heavy-chain constant region","The hinge region"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heavy- and light-chain variable regions jointly form the antigen-binding site at the tip of each arm. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 39, 41, 42"},{"q":"What recruits smooth muscle cells into the developing lesion?","choices":["Direct contact with circulating low-density lipoprotein","Factors released from activated platelets and macrophages","Mechanical stretch from the pulse pressure alone","Complement deposition on the endothelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and once recruited they proliferate, produce extracellular matrix, and T cells are drawn in &mdash; the lesion becomes self-sustaining. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Fibrous encasement of the heart developing over years","Adhesion of the heart to surrounding mediastinal structures","External compression of the chambers by pericardial fluid","Failure of the ventricle to contract because of inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The heart is prevented from filling from outside, so the muscle and valves may be entirely normal while the output collapses. Impairing filling. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 69"}]};
